Render each cursor's sizes concurrently, and give the intermediate SVG a
path of its own.

Upstream rasterises one rsvg-convert per (cursor, size, frame) and waits
for each in turn. That is ~970 processes at the 19 sizes this machine
builds, and process startup rather than rendering is what the run is made
of: 10s serial against 1.4s with the same work in flight. The theme is
rebuilt on every wallpaper change, so that difference is the whole
question of whether the cursor can follow the wallpaper inline.

The temp path is part of the same change and would be worth making on its
own. Upstream recolours into a fixed `/tmp/.cursor.0248.svg`, which two
concurrent conversions overwrite under each other -- and two wallpaper
changes in quick succession are two concurrent runs. Writing it beside
the PNG it feeds makes it per-cursor and per-run, and it is unlinked
after the process that reads it has exited rather than immediately.

Output is byte-identical to the serial build.

